steps.body:
A risk system keys positions by (account, instrument) and a quote cache by (symbol, venue), and every lookup costs as much as the hash lets it. `std::unordered_map<K, V>` hashes keys with `std::hash<K>`, a function object from `<functional>`: create one and call it, as in `std::hash<std::string>{}("AAPL")`, to get a `std::size_t`. The library defines it for integers, floating point numbers, pointers, strings, and a few more types, but never for your own structs.

In libstdc++, `std::hash` of an integer returns the integer itself. That identity is the cheapest hash there is, and `std::unordered_map` gets away with it because its bucket count is prime. Prices that are all multiples of 100 still spread out, since 100 shares no factor with those primes:

```cpp
std::cout << std::hash<int>{}(19275) << '\n';   // 19275
std::unordered_map<long long, int> level_by_price;
for (int k = 1; k <= 1000; ++k) {
    level_by_price[k * 100] = k;
}
std::size_t used = 0;
for (std::size_t b = 0; b < level_by_price.bucket_count(); ++b) {
    used += level_by_price.bucket_size(b) > 0;
}
std::cout << used << " of " << level_by_price.bucket_count() << " buckets used\n";   // 1000 of 1109
```

The identity is libstdc++'s choice, not the standard's. MSVC hashes integers with FNV-1a and sizes its buckets in powers of two. A power-of-two table keeps only the low bits of the hash, and the next part shows identity going wrong there.

A key with several fields, like (symbol, venue), needs `operator==` and a hash. Specialize `std::hash` for it inside `namespace std`, which the standard allows for your own types, and the map picks it up by itself. Or pass any struct with that `operator()` as the map's third template argument. Combine the fields so each one changes every bit of the result, the way Boost's `hash_combine` does:

```cpp harness
{{code}}
std::cout << (QuoteKey{"AAPL", 1} == QuoteKey{"AAPL", 1}) << ' ' << std::hash<QuoteKey>{}({"AAPL", 1}) % 1000 << '\n';
```

```cpp
struct QuoteKey {
    std::string symbol;
    int venue;
};

bool operator==(const QuoteKey& a, const QuoteKey& b) {
    return a.venue == b.venue && a.symbol == b.symbol;
}

namespace std {
template <>
struct hash<QuoteKey> {
    size_t operator()(const QuoteKey& k) const noexcept {
        size_t h = hash<string>{}(k.symbol);
        h ^= hash<int>{}(k.venue) + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15ULL + (h << 6) + (h >> 2);
        return h;
    }
};
}  // namespace std
```

Leave a field out, or add the fields up, and many keys share a bucket. Every lookup then walks that bucket comparing keys with `==`, and counting those calls measures the damage exactly.

